  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This preprint is beginning to attract downstream scientific reuse: it has one documented citation-like reuse in another 2026 bioRxiv preprint, and the article record also shows a subsequent peer-reviewed publication version. The broader attention record is still sparse, so the clearest signal at present is that the work has started to enter the research conversation rather than public discourse.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Rath, Sharma, Mani, and Bergmann examine how stomatal setpoints and environmental responsiveness are shaped by developmental trajectories, addressing a core question in plant developmental biology and phenotypic plasticity. The article focuses on stomatal production as a mechanism through which plants balance gas exchange and water loss, and it situates that biology in the context of changing developmental and environmental conditions.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">In terms of impact, the record is still early but not empty. OpenAlex shows zero citations to the posted version, yet the reuse graph captures one downstream reuse in a 2026 preprint on climate-responsive stomatal plasticity in tomato. That kind of reuse suggests the work is already being used as conceptual or experimental groundwork by other researchers working on stomatal development and environmental responsiveness.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The publication trajectory also strengthens the story of scholarly uptake. The preprint has a later peer-reviewed version listed in the record, indicating that the work moved beyond the posted draft into journal publication. Alongside this, Europe PMC indexes the preprint record and reports citations there, reinforcing that the article is visible in biomedical literature infrastructures even though the OpenAlex citation count for the posted version remains at zero.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Other signals are limited. There are no recorded online mentions in the available event-data sources, and the public-discourse fields are unavailable in the archive. Taken together, the evidence points to a technically focused plant biology study that is starting to be reused by peers, with its influence still concentrated in the research literature rather than in broader public channels.</p>",
